## The frontier is heading straight here

Several very recent papers line up almost one-for-one with what we’ve been circling.

**EXG: Self-Evolving Agents with Experience Graphs** argues that ordinary reflection and unstructured memory are insufficient, and instead stores successes **and failures** in a relational experience graph that can be reused online and offline. It reports better quality/efficiency than simpler memory/reflection approaches. ([arXiv][1])

**Scaling Teams or Scaling Time?** finds that adding more agents isn't necessarily the best way to improve a system: smaller teams with good lifelong memory can outperform bigger teams because they reuse accumulated experience. That's essentially evidence for “worker quality is not model count/model quality.” ([arXiv][2])

**Governed Collaborative Memory** goes even closer to our Lab design. It explicitly distinguishes **agent-local memory, shared institutional memory, archive memory and project-continuity memory**, and argues that the key problem becomes deciding which experiences deserve promotion into shared organizational state. ([arXiv][3])

**MUSE-Autoskill** treats skills as long-lived assets with creation, memory, management, evaluation and refinement, and even gives individual skills their own accumulated experience. ([arXiv][4])

And **Rethinking Self-Evolving Agent Skills**, published just weeks ago, found something extremely important for us: skill evolution is **sparse**, not a smooth “agent gets smarter every run” process. Only a minority of candidate changes actually establish a better validation result, and failed trajectories are particularly useful—but only when the resulting changes are validation-filtered. ([arXiv][5])

That validates exactly why `cg` matters.

```text
experience
    ↓
candidate lesson
    ↓
DON'T JUST BELIEVE IT
    ↓
test it
    ↓
promote / reject
```

There's also an entire current literature forming around the problem you just identified with rejected submissions: **credit assignment**. Q-Evolve starts from the fact that long-running agents often receive only a delayed final reward, so it's difficult to know which steps deserve blame/credit; it tries to infer denser process-level signals from trajectories. A 2026 survey calls this one of the defining challenges of agentic learning. ([arXiv][6])

So your intuition is correct:

> **“Submission rejected” is an extremely weak learning signal.**

