# A rejection should not mean “learn this was bad”

Take your example:

```text
TASK

"Generate 15 compelling x402 ideas."
```

An excellent worker might do:

```text
read task
   ↓
retrieve previous ideation experience
   ↓
buy current x402 Briefing
   ↓
inspect prior winning idea patterns
   ↓
generate 100 ideas
   ↓
deduplicate / cluster
   ↓
test novelty
   ↓
score feasibility
   ↓
specialist peer review
   ↓
select diverse top 25
   ↓
adversarial review
   ↓
select final 15
   ↓
write rationale
   ↓
submit
```

Suppose it loses.

You know only:

```text
OUTCOME = NOT_SELECTED
```

You **do not** know:

```text
idea generation was bad
research was bad
selection was bad
judge had different taste
someone else submitted better ideas
50 other entries were similarly excellent
timing mattered
sponsor wanted something else
```

So Hydra should record:

```text
external_outcome:
  rejected

information_value:
  LOW
```

Do **not** immediately mutate the Letta worker based on that.

This is precisely the sparse-credit problem current research is wrestling with. ([arXiv][7])

Instead, you manufacture better signals.

