# New agents can absolutely learn from shared runs — but not magically

This deserves a very precise distinction.

You should **not** do:

```text
new worker
    ↓
dump 100,000 old trajectories into context
```

And I also wouldn't store/private-share hidden model reasoning.

Instead, store observable experience:

```text
task
environment
inputs
tools called
sources used
artifacts
decisions/structured state
reviews
cost
evaluation
outcome
skills/memory versions
```

Hydra owns the organization-wide experience graph.

When a new Letta worker faces a task:

```text
new task
   ↓
LabContext.query(task)
   ↓
Hydra finds:
- similar runs
- successful procedures
- common failures
- useful Briefings
- good reviewers
- worker/task matches
   ↓
distill into ~2k useful tokens
   ↓
Letta worker receives it
```

So the new worker can inherit:

> “Our Lab has attempted 73 similar tasks. This workflow performs best; these three mistakes recur; Reviewer-7 is highly predictive.”

without inheriting every worker's entire personal memory.

That is **institutional knowledge**.

The current shared-memory research is explicitly discovering that this needs governance: provenance, scope, contradiction handling and decisions about which memories become institutional state. ([arXiv][8])

That is exactly Hydra's role in our architecture.

